The padding of a client's coalesced datagram put where quic-go puts it (hysteria client).

A client's handshake datagram carries an Initial packet and, behind it, a Handshake packet and a
1RTT one. quic-go packs the payloads of all three first, then writes them: the Initial packet gets
maxPacketSize minus the size of all of them as PADDING, and the two behind it get none
(vendor/quic-go/packet_packer.go:347-356, :373-441, :470-472).

Upstream ngtcp2 writes and seals each packet as it goes, so the leading Initial packet cannot know
what will follow it. Its padding therefore travels the other way: conn_should_pad_pkt leaves the
Initial packet unpadded when there is something to send at a later level, the REQUIRE_PADDING flag
is handed down the datagram, and the LAST packet pads it to size.

Both datagrams are 1280 bytes, and the difference is in the open. The Length field of a long header
is not encrypted, so the third datagram of the handshake reads, through a quic-go server's own qlog:

    reference   Initial 1080 · Handshake 79 · 1RTT 103
    ours        Initial   22 · Handshake 80 · 1RTT 1158

This patch composes that datagram the way round quic-go composes it. While a client writes a
datagram an Initial packet is going to lead, NGTCP2_CONN_FLAG_DEFER_INITIAL_PKT holds that packet
back; conn_write_deferred_initial then writes it into what the others left and moves it in front of
them, and conn_should_pad_pkt pads it because it leads the datagram rather than because nothing
follows. NGTCP2_COALESCED_INITIAL_RESERVE is what this costs and quic-go does not pay: the packets
written first are kept out of the room the Initial packet may need, because in quic-go's order the
Initial packet takes its room first. Anything it does not use it pads with, so the datagram is the
same size either way.

The padding is asked for with a flag of its own rather than with REQUIRE_PADDING, and that is not
tidiness. REQUIRE_PADDING also suppresses the check that drops an empty packet
(`if (pkt_empty && !require_padding)`), so an Initial packet with nothing to say became a packet of
padding — and since writing it neither emptied a queue nor discarded the packet number space, the
client wrote another, and another. Measured before the flag existed: 854 510 Initial packets into a
quic-go server that had answered the first one. NGTCP2_WRITE_PKT_FLAG_PAD_LEADING_INITIAL says only
"you lead the datagram", and an Initial packet with nothing to carry is still not written.

What this patch does NOT change is where the PADDING sits inside the Initial packet: quic-go writes
it between the ACK and the frames (packet_packer.go:1064-1066), ngtcp2 writes it after the frames, and
the whole payload is inside the AEAD — nothing outside the connection can tell the two apart.
Matching it would mean measuring the packet's own frames before serialising them, which is the
two-phase packing quic-go has and ngtcp2 does not.

The CONNECTION_CLOSE path composes its own coalesced datagram (conn_write_connection_close) and is
left alone: this client only closes after the handshake, where the Initial packet number space is
long discarded and the datagram holds a single 1RTT packet.

Applies on top of 0006.

